From cc02a903de68f90247e1f798244dea6a71f807f2 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: z060142 <z060142@yahoo.com.tw>
Date: Mon, 14 Sep 2026 03:52:55 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 202/214] feat: material types at the ray traced hit - shading
 tags and the extras record (CPU, decision 10)

The 4 texel material record could only describe a plain opaque Illum surface. Anything a CE material
actually does beyond that - a detail map, a blend layer, leaf transmittance, skin translucency, a
glass tint - simply did not reach the pool, so the reflection of a detail mapped wall, a tree or a
glass pane was a flat, untextured, opaque approximation of it.

Shading tag (matInfo3.z). RT_ShadingTag tags by shader, which is what selects the lighting code on
the primary surface: terrain (hit type or eST_Terrain) 5, Vegetation.cfx with alpha test 0.25,
HumanSkin.cfx 2, Glass.cfx 3, everything else 0. HumanSkin has to go by shader NAME - it declares
"ShaderType = General" exactly like Illum, so EShaderType cannot tell the two apart. 0.25 keeps its
old meaning and its old condition, so every material the current consumer already handled gets a
byte identical record. Tags 4 (Water) and 6 (EmissiveOnly) are reserved and never produced: water is
decision 09's, and CE has no usable "emissive but unlit" flag (MTL_FLAG_LIGHTING is set by no shipped
.mtl and read by no renderer code, so keying a tag off it would mis-tag the whole level).

Extras record. A material now occupies 1, 2 or 3 consecutive records: the base record, an optional
4 texel extras record at base + 1, and - for a %BLENDLAYER material - a full second base record for
the blend layer's own texture set at base + 2. The extras reference lives in the previously unused
low ExtractUint2 field of matInfo0.z. It is a RELATIVE offset, not an absolute record index, because
that field caps at 4095 while a static chunk can start anywhere in a million record pool.

  +0  detail: PackUint2(detailSlot+1, 0), tiling U, tiling V, PackTC16(diffuseScale, bumpScale)
  +1  blend:  absolute blend base record, EFTT_OPACITY slice+1, BlendFactor, BlendFalloff
  +2  transmittance rgb, strength
  +3  glass tint strength / skin SSS index, cloudiness / translucency, alpha blend opacity,
      PackTC16(BlendMaskTiling, BlendLayer2Tiling)

Every field is taken from the place the shader takes it: the detail tiling from the
EFTT_DETAIL_OVERLAY texture modificator that fills CM_DetailTilingAndAlphaRef, the scales and the
blend and tint parameters from the material's public shader params by name (with the shader's own
default when a material does not override one), the vegetation transmittance from TransmittanceColor
x BackDiffuseMultiplier, the skin transmittance from HumanSkin's exp((1-t) * (-8,-40,-64)).

EFTT_OPACITY carries two meanings selected by the tag - blend mask for Illum, translucency map for
leaves and skin - because that is exactly what CE does: Illum's BlendTex and Vegetation's and
HumanSkin's opacityTex are all TM_Opacity.

A blend layer is only recognised on Illum and Vegetation. EFTT_CUSTOM is the wrinkle map on HumanSkin
and the tint colour map on Glass, so the slot alone must never be read as "has a blend layer".

CheckStoreTextureInPool gains two optional parameters so the blend layer's Bump2Tex
(EFTT_CUSTOM_SECONDARY) can be encoded exactly like a _ddna normal slice with EFTT_DECAL_OVERLAY -
Illum's smoothness2Tex - donating the alpha, which is where the consumer reads smoothness from.

Extras are only allocated for materials that need them; a plain opaque Illum material with no detail
map is still a single record. The BVH status line reports "extras N, blend M", and the UV scale
mismatch check now also covers the detail and opacity slices.

Vertex colours: SRayHitVertex does carry a ColorB, but the triangle record has no lane for it and
none was added. %VERTCOLORS, %DIRTLAYER and the per-vertex half of the blend mask are therefore not
reproduced; the consumer substitutes 1 for vtxAlpha. Documented in the report, not silently wrong.

The self test round trips a synthetic material with a detail map, a blend layer and a transmittance
colour through RT_AppendMatRecordSet and the relocation the static builder performs, and decodes it
back with the CPU mirrors of the consumer's helpers; the verdict line now requires 0 mismatches
there too.

Cry3DEngine builds 0 errors / 0 warnings (Profile, bundled CMake 3.22). The engine was not run and
nothing was deployed. Report: scene-notes/rt/reports/05a-material-extras-cpu.md.

